00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:22.070 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  thank you for visiting  dare to know chess in the age of reason  one of our summer exhibitions here at  the world chess hall of fame  the show is inspired by chess in the age  of enlightenment  and particularly how chess was part of  a really lively scene of thinkers  writers philosophers  who are all passionate about the game of  chess chess had a really special part in  coffee house culture and coffee houses  were where people would go  either to play chess read discuss with  others the kind of events of the day  kind of the beginning of the public  sphere in this case  we have artifacts related to the cafe de  la rechans and in the center  is a really beautiful chess set called  the region's chess set  after the cafe the cafe de la regence  was the most famous  chess coffee house in paris in the 18th  century  and we have books by some of the writers  and chess players who are associated  with the lively chess scene there  we also have this print which shows  people huddled around a chess board  eagerly watching the next move of the  game as some of the early stars of chess  are playing including philidor who is  not only a chess player but he is also a  composer  and his portrait is on the opera in  paris  we have this case which shows not only a  mysterious  musical chess set at the end but also  other games that were played at the time  including  this beautiful tarot card set  many of the artifacts in this collection  or in this exhibition are from the  collection of tom gallegos  and he's a collector of antique games as  well as chess sets  we also have this freemasons apron  and if you look carefully at the floor  it has a chess board pattern  familiar black and white squares  this is a really special highlight of  dare to know  it's a chess head that once belonged to  madame tussaud  and we have a letter on display from her  grandson  attesting to its um provenance as her  own set  ma'am to soda is best known today for  her wax museum in london but she lived  many years in paris where she was  associated with royalty  and then when the french revolution  happened  her own life was in danger but she  ultimately  was saved and then she had the kind of  gruesome job of  cataloging the faces of many of her  friends who were sent to execution she  left france later and settled in london  where she rose to fame as the  proprietors  of the wax museum  the mechanical turk or the chess  automaton was a famous illusion  from the enlightenment era and it was  presented as though it were a chess  playing robot when in reality there was  a person  inside the cabinet controlling the moves  of the chessboard  people were allowed to challenge the  turk  and you can see in this little  reproduction  they would open the cabinet doors at the  beginning almost like um  you know a magic trick where the  magician's going to cut saw a woman in  half and they throw up in the cabinet  and they say nothing underneath nothing  under here  and then they close everything up and he  would  play against dignitaries  people in royal courts he traveled  throughout europe  and then ultimately to the united states  and captured the imagination of people  around the world and writers would  kind of challenge themselves to try to  figure out what was really going on  with the mechanical turk so you can see  in these illustrations  some different ways that people thought  that someone was hiding in the cabinet  including that someone was inside this  representation of the mechanical turk  himself  almost like a costume moving the arms  as you know the game would progress  and we'll finish here with the  encyclopedia chess set  the encyclopedia was a really unique  project which encapsulated the goals of  the enlightenment era  and in it the authors sought to catalog  all of the information in the world a  very humble aim  and so it was everything from the newest  political ideas to  articles about different professions and  in this case  this chess set is inspired by an article  from about toy makers  in france tableteers and so this is a  pattern that shows  a really common style of chess set at  the time  represented in the show by this one  and so it kind of like became associated  with the encyclopedia  which was written by denise detero and  his collaborators  he and his collaborators were  often subject to persecution  for their ideas and sometimes had to go  into hiding  and so this chessa even though it looks  pretty humble has a really important  place in history  thank you for visiting our virtual  version of dare to know chess in the age  of reason  and i hope that we'll be able to see you  in st louis soon at one of our  exhibitions

